From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:31:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: charge the geometry heap to a pool its device's
 creator shares

A Venus host grants a guest's GPU a share of memory and refuses a guest
allocation that would take the process past it, and a refused allocation is
fatal to the guest's application. The geometry heap is memory the driver
allocates for itself, so nothing charged it to that share: it grew to
128 MiB, 240 MiB at once with the heaps a recording still held, however
little of the share was left, and a guest allocation made while it grew
could pass the same check and take the process past the share together
with it.

A device can now be created with a device-memory pool, a driver-private
structure chained to VkDeviceCreateInfo and declared in
kk_device_memory_pool.h: callbacks that reserve bytes, release a
reservation, and report the pool's headroom. A pooled device reserves a
heap's size before creating the heap and releases the reservation once
Metal counts the heap, so a heap and allocations charged to the same pool
never pass its limit together. Its heaps:

- take at most half of the pool's headroom, so the host's conservative
  bound, which overstates a draw at levels the TCS computes by up to 4096
  times, never leaves the guest nothing; a device's first heap may take
  1 MiB whatever is left, so its draws record and the GPU reports their
  need;
- grow on a bound no further than the 128 MiB cap, but on what the GPU
  counted draws were refused as far as the pool allows, up to 4095 MiB and
  the largest Metal buffer, where a device without a pool keeps the cap;
- when the pool refuses one, leave the device the heap it has, or none until
  the pool has room, instead of stopping its growth as Metal's refusal does;
- are freed when no recording holds them, they grew past the starting size
  and the pool's headroom has fallen below half their size, and the next
  draw that needs a heap creates one at what it then needs.

A device created without a pool behaves as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
